      "writtenOn": "2020-10-07T14:47:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Normally when you construct a file in incfs, you mark it as having a merkle tree, stream the file then enable verity. Merkle tree validation is then handled entirely by incfs itself.However, there is also a use case when we are needing to protect a file which does not come with the merkle tree but does live in an incfs mount. So in that case, there is no hash tree when verity is enabled. The cleanest solution would seem to be to build the incfs style merkle tree then enable the incfs protection.\n\nSo now I\u0027m wondering if this isn\u0027t simply a duplicate of verity code, and if I change the fs-verity support so that vops-\u003eget_root_hash can return \u0027No\u0027 then we fall through to the fs-verity code that builds the tree.",
      "parentUuid": "1542fa68_989057ac",
      "revId": "7175461df5b351b97230d2168fbb458193edbb98",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
